  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:Th9rFZSbMHM",
    "purpose": "A brand deep dive into Crown Northampton, a small family-owned English sneaker maker. It is episode two of the creator's 'world's most fantastic' manufacturer series. Using the Harlestone as the main example and other models to illustrate the brand's pillars, it explains the materials, construction and heritage behind the sneakers.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Crown Northampton's sneakers are defined by fifth-generation shoemaker Chris Woodford's heritage and expertise. That shows in premium materials, comfort, whole cut construction and simplicity, and the Harlestone shows each of them.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The Harlestone came out of the Covid-19 pandemic: hand-sewn moccasin-style uppers let half the workforce stitch at home, which slowed production, so Chris chose not to cut costs on materials.",
      "Crown Northampton is run by Chris Woodford of E. Woodford & Sons, a 116-year-old, fifth-generation shoemaking business in Northampton, England, historically one of the world's great shoemaking hubs.",
      "The four pillars of the brand are premium materials, softness and comfort, whole cut construction, and simplicity.",
      "The Jazz collection, including the Regent, was the brand's first design and is popular in Japan, where customers call the shoes 'marshmallows' for their softness.",
      "The Harlestone uses Italian vegetable-tanned baby calf uppers and hand-poured natural latex soles from France with heritage protections. Its lining is oak bark tanned leather from the UK's last traditional oak bark tannery.",
      "Shell cordovan is explained: two shells per horse, a tight grain that rolls rather than creases, rich patina, durability and easy upkeep.",
      "The Abbey uses Dearborn leather that is semi-split, so a stiffener can be slipped inside and no lining is needed. The idea came from a leather-splitting demonstration Chris saw 25 years earlier.",
      "Whole cut construction, one flawless piece of leather wrapped around the last with a single back seam, carries over from the E. Woodford & Sons bespoke division into boots like the Everdon Rambler."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Covid-19 design solution",
        "summary": "Cold open on how the pandemic led Chris Woodford to design the Harlestone around hand-sewn uppers that employees could stitch at home, and why that pushed materials upmarket. Covers Northampton's shoemaking history and the series intro.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Exploring the brand lineup",
        "summary": "Introduces why the episode looks at several Crown Northampton models, with a no-sponsorship disclaimer and a Maine update, then starts with the Regent from the Jazz collection, nicknamed 'marshmallows' in Japan.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Brand pillars and features",
        "summary": "Lays out the four things that define Crown Northampton: premium materials, softness and comfort, whole cut construction and simplicity. The Jazz collection is presented as the centre of the brand, with comfort as its highlight.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Sponsor spotlight: Asket",
        "summary": "Sponsor segment on Asket's raw denim in regular and loose fits. Covers the brand's functional minimalism, its non-selvedge organic Japanese denim, bartacks instead of rivets, and Italian manufacturing.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "Materials of the Harlston",
        "summary": "Explains the roughly $800 Harlestone's Italian vegetable-tanned baby calf uppers and its hand-poured natural latex soles, made without machines and protected as French cultural heritage.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Historic tanning methods",
        "summary": "Covers the lining leather from J & FJ Baker & Co., the UK's last traditional oak bark tannery, why Northampton's location made it a shoe capital, and Chris's reasons for using this leather.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "The magic of cordovan",
        "summary": "Explains shell cordovan: where it comes from, its limited yield, its tight grain that rolls instead of creasing, patina, durability and easy maintenance, and why it has a cult following.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "Innovation in leather",
        "summary": "Looks at the Abbey's unlined Dearborn leather upper. A semi-split technique lets a stiffener sit inside the leather without a separate lining, an idea from a demonstration Chris saw 25 years earlier.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 15
      },
      {
        "title": "Heritage and craftsmanship",
        "summary": "Explains whole cut construction using the E. Woodford & Sons bespoke division and the Everdon Rambler boot, and how the boot reflects Chris's own heritage rather than copying other bootmakers. Ends with closing thoughts.",
        "first_segment": 15,
        "last_segment": 17
      }
    ],
